--TEST--
`echo` and a `Core\Response` body member writing one response body is a compile error, in either order, and one writer is a whole body however often it writes -- `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`'s sixth row: the two writers disagree about the body's type and
// its `Content-Type`, and silently letting the last one win is how a JSON
// endpoint acquires an HTML prelude. § 3 binds `echo` by context, so what is
// refused is a `#[Route]` handler -- the one body ADR 0102 § 1 makes a request
// statically certain to reach.
class Pages
{
    #[Core\Route(path: "/report", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function report(): void
    {
        echo "<h1>Report</h1>";
        Core\Response::json(1);
    }

    // The other order is the same mistake and takes the same refusal: what is
    // wrong is that the body has two writers, not that one of them came second.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/receipt", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function receipt(): void
    {
        Core\Response::text("receipt");
        echo " and a footer";
    }

    // A closure written in the handler writes the same response, so the two
    // writers meet there too -- its body is checked inside the handler's.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/deferred", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function deferred(): void
    {
        echo "<h1>Deferred</h1>";
        var $write = fn (): void => { Core\Response::text("later"); };
    }

    // The accepted half, so a body that stops being accepted fails here rather
    // than as a missing refusal. `echo` alone is § 4's last bullet -- the
    // inline-HTML page, which means `text/html` -- and writing it twice is
    // still one writer.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/page", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function page(): void
    {
        echo "<h1>Page</h1>";
        echo "<p>one writer, twice</p>";
    }

    // And a reader is not a writer: `Core\Server::isDraining` says nothing
    // about the body, so it is an ordinary handler beside an `echo`.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/health", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function health(): void
    {
        bool $draining = Core\Server::isDraining();
        echo $draining;
    }
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0801]: a response has one body, and this handler writes it with both `echo` and `Core\Response::json`
   --> case.nvs:13:9
%A
error[E0801]: a response has one body, and this handler writes it with both `Core\Response::text` and `echo`
   --> case.nvs:23:9
%A
error[E0801]: a response has one body, and this handler writes it with both `echo` and `Core\Response::text`
   --> case.nvs:33:9
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
